 | `body`   | string   | 0–10,000 chars                                                   | `''`    | yes             |
 | `status` | enum     | `draft` \| `published`                                           | `draft` | yes             |
 | `tags`   | string[] | each: lowercase `a-z0-9-`, 1–30 chars; at most 10; no duplicates | `[]`    | yes             |
+| `ownerUserId` | uuid | the owning user; set by the server from the caller's identity (see R6) | —  | **no** (server-owned) |
 
 ## Rules
 
@@ -29,10 +30,39 @@ go in the service.
 | R4  | a `published` note cannot go back to `draft`                                                            | service (needs stored state)       | `RuleViolationError` → 422 |
 | R5  | publishing through PATCH requires the resulting `body` (patch value, else stored value) to be non-empty | service (needs stored state)       | `RuleViolationError` → 422 |
 
+| R6  | every note has an owner: over REST the signed-in user, over MCP the calling device's owner. A client cannot set or change it | service (from the caller's identity, never the payload) | a sent `ownerUserId` is stripped by the schema |
+| R7  | a caller sees and changes only their own notes: another user's note is **404** for GET, PATCH and DELETE (never 403, so ids cannot be probed) and is absent from every list | service (`findOne(ownerId, id)`, as `DevicesService`) | `NotFoundError` → 404 / MCP tool error `NOT_FOUND` |
+
 > R5 was added while writing this spec. R2 alone left a hole: create a draft with an
 > empty body, then PATCH it to `published`. This is the kind of gap an agent should
 > raise as a question, not quietly close with a rule of its own.
 
+## MCP tools
+
+| tool          | over Streamable HTTP (device-authenticated)                     | over stdio (no device identity)        |
+| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
+| `my-notes`    | the calling device's owner's notes, `{ items, total }`          | tool error `RULE_VIOLATION: No device identity…` |
+| `list-notes`  | the same notes, paged (`limit`, `offset`); kept from chapter 06 | same tool error                        |
+| `get-note`    | one of the owner's notes; anyone else's is `NOT_FOUND`          | same tool error                        |
+| `create-note` | creates a note owned by the device's owner                      | same tool error                        |
+
+**Decision (R6 over stdio):** stdio carries no identity, so there is no owner to give a new
+note and no owner to scope a read to. The notes tools **fail closed** there, with the same
+tool error `whoami` already returns. The alternative (stdio as an all-seeing local admin)
+would need ownerless notes or an invented owner, both of which break R6.
+
+## Open questions (raised, not guessed)
+
+- **Q1: deleting a user who owns notes.** Devices block it (FK `RESTRICT` → 409). Notes are
+  in memory, so nothing blocks it and the notes become unreachable orphans. Block (409),
+  cascade, or leave as is? Not changed by this lab.
+- **Q2: notes created before ownership existed.** None survive a restart (memory storage),
+  so there is nothing to migrate. If notes move to Postgres, the column needs the chapter 04
+  treatment (nullable → decide → `SET NOT NULL`).
+- **Q3: a stdio identity.** Should a local operator be able to name a user (an env var) so
+  stdio can use the notes tools? Not done: it would be an unauthenticated way to act as any user.
+
 ## Out of scope
 
-Authentication, persistence beyond memory, search.
+Authentication (provided by `BasicAuthGuard` and the MCP device middleware), persistence
+beyond memory, search, sharing notes between users.
